---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="Security - Elo" activeNav="security">
  <main class="blog-panel">
    <article class="blog-content">
      <header class="page-header centered">
        <h1>Security</h1>
        <p class="page-intro">How we think about Elo security, what we aim for, what you must provide, and our bug bounty program</p>
      </header>

      <section class="about-section">
        <h2>Secure by Design</h2>
        <p>
          Elo is built for a specific use case: letting people write small programs that run somewhere they don't control.
          Think of a spreadsheet formula, but more powerful. You write it, someone else's computer runs it.
        </p>
        <p>
          That's why Elo is a <strong>pure expression language</strong>: no loops, no recursion, no <code>eval</code>,
          no I/O, no access to host globals. It's a calculator — you give it data, it gives you a result.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section">
        <h2>It depends who writes the code, and whose data it runs on</h2>
        <p>
          Elo is not one tool with one security posture. The posture is set by <em>trust</em>: whether the Elo
          expression comes from someone you trust, and whether the input data is something you trust. "Trustworthy
          code over trustworthy data" is a portable calculator; "untrustworthy code over arbitrary data" is a
          hostile-input compiler. Same language, very different stakes.
        </p>
        <table class="trust-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Elo code</th><th>Input data</th><th>What Elo is here</th><th>What you rely on</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>trusted</td><td>trusted</td>
              <td>a portable expression evaluator</td>
              <td>correctness; a sandbox is good hygiene</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>untrusted</strong></td><td>trusted</td>
              <td>user formulas over your data</td>
              <td>Elo's sandbox <em>plus</em> your resource limits</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>trusted</td><td><strong>arbitrary</strong></td>
              <td>your logic over foreign data</td>
              <td>the data contract (data in, not host objects)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>untrusted</strong></td><td><strong>arbitrary</strong></td>
              <td>fully hostile input</td>
              <td>everything below, at once</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          We design, document, and triage for the hard end — <strong>untrusted code</strong> — so the easy end is
          covered for free. Everything below assumes someone hostile may write the expression.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section">
        <h2>What Elo aims for</h2>
        <p>
          Elo touches your systems at two moments: <strong>compile time</strong>, when the compiler turns an
          expression into target code (JavaScript, Ruby, Python, SQL), and <strong>runtime</strong>, when that code
          runs over your data. The properties below are what we <em>design</em> Elo to hold at both, and what the
          bug bounty pays for: we treat a violation as a bug and fix it. They are goals we work hard to meet — not a
          warranty. Elo is open-source software provided <em>as is</em> (see the
          <a href="https://github.com/enspirit/elo/blob/main/LICENSE">license</a>).
        </p>

        <h3>At compile time</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>It always finishes.</strong> No expression — however deeply nested or malformed — makes the compiler hang or overflow its stack.</li>
          <li><strong>It can't be turned against the compiler.</strong> Compiling an expression never executes attacker code <em>inside the compiler process</em>: no <code>eval</code>, no loading of host code, no attacker-controlled method firing during compilation.</li>
          <li><strong>An import reads a file, and only reads it.</strong> An <code>import</code> is the one capability compilation has: the compiler reads the <code>.elo</code> file the expression names, parses it as Elo and inlines it. Nothing in it runs at compile time. The loader is an argument, not a built-in — a host that compiles expressions it does not trust gives none, and an import is then refused. The playground and the <code>parse()</code> API do exactly that; <code>eloc</code> and <code>elo</code> read the filesystem, as the tools of whoever runs them.</li>
          <li><strong>It never crashes uncontrollably.</strong> Bad input yields a typed Elo error, not an uncaught native exception.</li>
          <li><strong>It emits only what you wrote.</strong> No value threaded through the compiler — a name, a key, a literal — can break out of its slot and inject code into the output. This holds whether the expression came from source text or from an AST handed to the compiler API.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>At runtime, by construction</h3>
        <p>The code Elo emits is a pure function of its input. By construction it <strong>cannot</strong>:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>call <code>eval</code> or build code at runtime — what you see is what runs;</li>
          <li>read files, open sockets, read environment variables, or reach host globals (<code>window</code>, <code>process</code>, …);</li>
          <li>reach host-runtime internals, such as the JavaScript prototype chain or the <code>Function</code> constructor.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section">
        <h2>What you must provide</h2>
        <p>
          Elo gives you a <strong>capability sandbox</strong> — but not a <strong>resource sandbox</strong>. Two
          things are yours to handle, and skipping them is how untrusted expressions hurt you.
        </p>
        <p class="safe-callout">
          → Read <a href="/running-safely"><strong>Running Elo safely</strong></a> — a practical guide with
          copy-paste reference sandboxes for Node, the browser, Ruby, Python, and PostgreSQL.
        </p>

        <h3>Run compiled Elo under a memory limit and a timeout</h3>
        <p>
          An Elo expression has no loops, but it can still be <em>expensive</em>: it can build a large value from the
          data you give it, or chain enough work to take real CPU. We make a best effort to minimize this — the
          primitives that let a <em>short</em> expression allocate out of proportion are bounded (array repeat is
          rejected; string repeat and padding, and the array a <code>patch</code> grows to reach an index, are capped
          by the <code>ELO_MAX_STRING_LENGTH</code> environment variable, default 10,000,000) — but bounding
          primitives does not bound total cost, and we will
          never claim it does. <strong>Run compiled Elo the way you would run any untrusted computation: in an
          isolated context, under a memory ceiling and a wall-clock timeout.</strong> See
          <a href="/running-safely">Running Elo safely</a> for copy-paste reference sandboxes for every target.
        </p>

        <h3>Compile untrusted expressions without a filesystem</h3>
        <p>
          An <code>import</code> names a file, and the file is read when the expression is
          <em>compiled</em>. So an expression you do not trust must not be compiled by something
          holding a filesystem: use <code>loadProgram</code> with no module loader, or with one of
          your own that serves only the modules you mean to offer, and any other import is refused
          with an error. Do not shell out to <code>eloc</code> on an expression a stranger wrote —
          that is a compiler with your whole filesystem in reach.
        </p>

        <h3>Pass data, not host objects</h3>
        <p>
          Elo operates on <strong>plain data</strong>: strings, numbers, booleans, null, lists, and maps whose keys
          are strings — the shape you get from parsing JSON. Do <em>not</em> hand it an arbitrary host object (an ORM
          row, a <code>Decimal</code>, a class instance with operator overloads). Elo compiles <code>_ + 1</code> or
          <code>Int(_)</code> to the target's own operators and built-ins; in Ruby and Python those dispatch to
          whatever methods the <em>object</em> defines, so a host object's own code can run — outside the sandbox.
          Convert host objects to plain data before evaluating.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section bounty-section">
        <h2>Bug Bounty Program</h2>
        <p>
          Found a security issue? We want to hear from you. We offer rewards for valid security vulnerabilities:
        </p>

        <div class="bounty-grid">
          <div class="bounty-card">
            <div class="bounty-amount">50&euro;</div>
            <div class="bounty-level">Medium Severity</div>
          </div>
          <div class="bounty-card">
            <div class="bounty-amount">100&euro;</div>
            <div class="bounty-level">High Severity</div>
          </div>
          <div class="bounty-card bonus">
            <div class="bounty-amount">2&times;</div>
            <div class="bounty-level">If you provide a fix</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3>In scope</h3>
        <p>Anything that lets an untrusted expression (or untrusted input) break one of the properties above:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Compiler code execution</strong> — an expression or AST that runs code inside the compiler process. <em>Critical.</em></li>
          <li><strong>Output injection</strong> — a value that escapes its slot in the emitted code. <em>Critical / High.</em></li>
          <li><strong>Runtime capability escape</strong> — emitted code that reaches <code>eval</code>, I/O, host globals, or host internals. <em>High / Critical.</em></li>
          <li><strong>Compile-time denial of service</strong> — input that hangs or crashes the compiler. <em>High.</em></li>
          <li><strong>Disproportionate allocation</strong> — a <em>short</em> expression that forces allocation grossly out of proportion to its input, via a stdlib primitive we failed to bound. <em>Medium / High.</em></li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Out of scope</h3>
        <p>By design — documented, not a vulnerability (though we welcome feedback if our guidance is unclear):</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A <strong>legal expression that is simply expensive</strong> over the data provided (large sort, deep chaining, big output). This is what your resource sandbox is for; we bound disproportionate primitives as a best effort, but general cost is not a bounty-eligible bug.</li>
          <li><strong>Resource exhaustion</strong> (out-of-memory, long run) that a memory-plus-timeout sandbox would contain.</li>
          <li>Passing <strong>non-data host objects</strong> as input and observing their methods run — a data-contract violation.</li>
          <li>Behaviour of your host wrapper or the runtime libraries Elo depends on, except where Elo emits a call that misuses them.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Rules</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Demonstrate the issue with a valid <code>.elo</code> program — or, for compiler-API injection, with the AST you build</li>
          <li>Explain the target environment where the vulnerability occurs</li>
          <li>Provide identifying information for payment (no anonymous submissions)</li>
          <li>Keep issues private for 90 days while we work on a fix</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Final severity is assessed by our team, against the categories above rather than a bare score. Valid
          in-scope reports are credited and, where applicable, assigned a CVE.
        </p>

        <h3>Contact</h3>
        <p>
          Send security reports to <a href="mailto:security@enspirit.dev">security@enspirit.dev</a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <Footer className="blog-footer" />
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .about-section ul {
    margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
  }

  .about-section li {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.8;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .about-section h3 {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .trust-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .trust-table th,
  .trust-table td {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .trust-table th {
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .trust-table tr.highlight td {
    background: var(--bg-secondary);
  }

  .safe-callout {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    background: var(--bg-secondary);
    padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
    border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
    margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .bounty-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .bounty-card {
    background: var(--bg-secondary);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .bounty-card.bonus {
    border-color: var(--accent);
  }

  .bounty-amount {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: bold;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .bounty-level {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .bounty-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .trust-table {
      font-size: 0.8rem;
    }
  }
</style>
